, update does the FIX. Then proceed. During the task: the moment a tool result is a dead-end (errored/no-value/bulky error output), call context_trim with that result's tool_use id immediately — leave a one-line conclusion and keep the clean worktable. Then proceed.","input":null}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":" ","signature":"gopi-empty-thinking","input":null},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_00_DBZNBw0aoTLUmCj4hrjW4694","name":"memory_search","input":{"query":"capital of France river Seine geography chain trivia"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","input":null,"tool_use_id":"call_00_DBZNBw0aoTLUmCj4hrjW4694","content":"[t+3s] [tool_use id: call_00_DBZNBw0aoTLUmCj4hrjW4694]\n#1380 法国地理链条:首都→河流→海→洋 (geography chain) tier=warm conf=0.90 lesson= when= eff_conf=0.91 tokens=6 fb=0.67(1)\nQ: France chain: what river runs through Paris, where does it empty, which ocean connects? 法国巴黎塞纳河英吉利海峡大西洋 链条题\nA: France → Paris → Seine river → English Channel (La Manche) → Atlantic Ocean. Paris lies on the Seine; the Seine empties into the English Channel at Le Havre; the Channel's western end opens into the Atlantic. The English Channel is a sea/bay, not a river mouth straight into ocean — treat La Manche as the intermediate sea linking Seine to Atlantic.\n\nkeywords: France, Paris, capital, Seine, river, English Channel, La Manche, Atlantic Ocean, 法国, 巴黎, 塞纳河, 英吉利海峡, 大西洋, geography chain, 首都 河流 海峡 大洋, capitals of Europe, which river Paris\n---\n#1381 France-Paris-Seine river chain geography tier=warm conf=0.85 lesson= when= tokens=6\nQ: What river flows through Paris, what sea does the Seine empty into, and what ocean connects?\nA: Paris lies on the Seine River; the Seine empties into the English Channel (La Manche); the English Channel connects to the Atlantic Ocean. Chain: France → Paris → Seine → English Channel → Atlantic Ocean. Note: the Seine meets the Channel via its estuary at Le Havre/Honfleur; the Channel (La Manche) links to the Atlantic at its western end past the Strait of Dover (Pas de Calais connects it to the North Sea in the east).\n\nkeywords: 法国, Paris, Seine, 塞纳河, English Channel, 英吉利海峡, La Manche, Atlantic Ocean, 大西洋, capital river sea ocean chain\n---\n#1172 gopi 三段式分支收编链（发现/试探/分流）实现配方 tier=warm conf=1.00 lesson= when= sources=feat/branch-adoption-chain @ 0c183cc,~/workspace/gopi-adoption-chain tokens=1 fb=0.85(5)\nQ: gopi serve tick 怎么自动收编 client push back 的分支？merge 冲突自动派解冲突任务的实现配方与坑？\nA: 三段式分支收编链（branch adoption chain）实现配方：①发现=git for-each-ref 双通道 refs/heads+refs/remotes（本地路径 remote 的 git push 直达 refs/heads 不产生 remote-tracking ref！调试时被坑）+台账 completed 任务 deliverable.branch 配对门（孤儿分支 skip）②试探=git merge --no-commit --no-ff（TryMerge seam）③a 干净=commit \"adoption: merge \u003cbranch\u003e (\u003ctip\u003e)\"+branch [-r] -d；③b 冲突=merge --abort 保存现场+TaskSubmit 自足 briefing（冲突文件 --diff-filter=U、逐文件 \u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c 块 40 行截断、master...branch --stat、台账 intent、验收标准），tags=merge-conflict,auto-dispatch 经 taskTagPriority 自动高优先 ④红线=Go 只做发现/试探/派发/收编 ⑤防风暴=payload-keyed 查重（payload LIKE '%branch \u003cname\u003e (tip %'）且查重在试探之前跑。serve 集成=serveAdoptionSweep() pass step（serveStaleSweep 之后），GOPI_ADOPTION_INTERVAL 默认 15min 0/负/junk=禁用，脏工作树拒绝。CLI=gopi task adopt [--repo --master --submit] 默认 report-only。坑：TaskUpdate 状态机 pending→completed 非法须过 in_progress；TaskSearch 只搜 summary+FTS 不含 payload（查 payload 要直开 DB SELECT）；CLI 子命令加 verb 三处=parseTasksArgs case+taskFlagMember case+runTasksSubcommand switch；cmd/agent 包名是 main 不是 agent。master 基线 be60518 上 go test ./internal/tools/ 全量 hang 超时（sql connectionOpener，master 既有）——验证用 -run 相关族。\n\nkeywords: 分支收编 branch adoption chain, 收编链, merge 自动收编 auto-merge branch, serve tick pass step, serveAdoptionSweep, GOPI_ADOPTION_INTERVAL 节流, refs/heads 直达 push 本地路径 remote, for-each-ref 双通道, remote-tracking ref 不产生, 冲突派任务 conflict dispatch briefing, merge-conflict tag 高优先, taskTagPriority, payload 查重 dedup 同分支一次, 幂等 re-tick, TaskSearch 不搜 payload, TaskUpdate 状态机 in_progress 中转, cli_tasks verb 三处注册, task_adoption.go, devdoc/branch-adoption-chain.md, feat/branch-adoption-chain\n---\n#1347 Manual task-run worktree recycle — three root causes + fix tier=warm conf=0.95 lesson= when= eff_conf=1.00 sources=GoPi epic t-1788397248,commit 319f063 tokens=1 fb=0.75(2)\nQ: Why did a manual gopi task run leave its worktree behind (and how was it fixed)? What were the three root causes?\nA: Root cause of 'manual gopi task run leaves its worktree behind' (epic t-1788397248) and the fix, for reuse: (1) The finish-cleanup chain TaskFinishCleanupSessionID was client-gated — `if IsServerNode() { skip }` returned before ANY worktree removal, so server/single-node (zpeer has no sync_peers) NEVER recycled. Fix: mode-split — IsServerNode() OR finishCleanupServerTarget()==\"\" (no peers) → new finishCleanupServerRecycle (worktree remove only, KEEP branch = merger input); client-with-peer → the existing push→remove→-d chain unchanged. (2) git worktree list --porcelain is MULTI-LINE — the chain's finishCleanupGit first-line summarizer truncated it, so the porcelain parsers (finishCleanupBranchWorktree, finishCleanupOwningWorktree) silently never matched a linked worktree. Fix: finishCleanupGitRaw raw-output reader; also the FIRST porcelain entry is the main checkout — never return it (skip on first). (3) The child's own end-hook chain runs on its AUTO row (no deliverable → skip); the WRAPPER row carries deliverable{repo,branch} but was closed by writeback with no chain call. Fix: delivery-time hook in runTaskRunOnce after completed writeback + mirror reconcile — the shared funnel for manual task run / task run next / worker round, so all recycle at delivery. Server arm guards: exempt branch (master etc), clean worktree (git status --porcelain — dirty = live bond skip), linked-worktree-on-branch resolution. File: internal/tools/task_finish_cleanup.go; devdoc/manual-taskrun-recycle-20260903.md.\n\nkeywords: manual task run worktree left behind, finish-cleanup client-gated, IsServerNode skip, server single-node never recycled, finishCleanupServerRecycle, worktree list porcelain multi-line truncation, first-line summarizer bug, finishCleanupGitRaw, wrapper row no chain call, runTaskRunOnce delivery-time hook, task run next worker round share funnel, worktree remove keep branch merger input, 分支保留合并输入, 工作树未回收根因\n---\n#224 CivRealm evidence: LLM agents vs heuristic AI in turn-based 4X games tier=warm conf=0.85 lesson= when= eff_conf=1.00 sources=arXiv:2401.10568 (CivRealm); arXiv:2305.16291 (Voyager); arXiv:2303.11366 (Reflexion) tokens=1\nQ: How well do LLM agents (GPT-4) perform playing Civilization VI via a text interface, and what are their failure modes?\nA: CivRealm (Meta FAIR, arXiv:2401.10568) built a text-based interface to Civilization VI and evaluated GPT-4 / ReAct-style agents against the game's heuristic AI. Results: LLM agents LOST DECISIVELY (often 2-3x score gap) despite perfect symbolic state. Three failure modes, in order: (1) long-term planning / temporal credit assignment — agent abandoned its own 50-turn-old goals because each prompt was fresh context; (2) resource/economy management — spammed unpayable units, misordered build queues, settled cities on bad tiles; (3) spatial reasoning — textual grid caused circular movement, ignored river defense bonuses, failed adjacency rules. Transferable lesson for any LLM-in-4X project (HoMM3/VCMI included): the bottleneck is cognition not perception; mitigate with persistent plan memory + reflection (Reflexion), skill libraries (Voyager), and engine-computed tools for pathfinding/danger instead of raw grids. Also: a non-vision LLM CAN drive a GUI 4X game through a text bridge — the modality is not the blocker.\n\nkeywords: CivRealm Civ VI LLM agent benchmark, GPT-4 lose strategy game heuristic AI, LLM 4X game failure long-term planning spatial, text interface turn-based strategy LLM, Voyager skill library Reflexion reflection game, LLM agent cognition not perception bottleneck"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":" ","signature":"gopi-empty-thinking","input":null},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_00_qGB2Ff4ls3y3c4aXyucS1083","name":"memory_feedback","input":{"id":1380,"rating":"useful"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_01_rHxdGKHZlWA5Xgu45V9R0987","name":"memory_feedback","input":{"id":1381,"rating":"useful"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","input":null,"tool_use_id":"call_00_qGB2Ff4ls3y3c4aXyucS1083","content":"[t+6s] [tool_use id: call_00_qGB2Ff4ls3y3c4aXyucS1083]\nMemory feedback recorded (id=1380, rating=useful): useful=2.00 useless=0.00 fb_score=+0.125 confidence_step=+0.05"},{"type":"tool_result","input":null,"tool_use_id":"call_01_rHxdGKHZlWA5Xgu45V9R0987","content":"[t+6s] [tool_use id: call_01_rHxdGKHZlWA5Xgu45V9R0987]\nMemory feedback recorded (id=1381, rating=useful): useful=1.00 useless=0.00 fb_score=+0.042 confidence_step=+0.05"}]}],"tools":[{"name":"read","description":"Read a file's contents as text. Use read — not shell cat/head/sed — to see any file before analyzing or editing it. Every page is capped at ~50KB; a truncated page ends with a footer naming the total line count and the next offset. Large file? Page with offset/limit — a full read burns tokens. NOTE offset semantics: offset=N skips the first N lines (continue after line K with offset=K) — NOT the 'start at line N' convention. If the path is a symlink, the read result notes the link target.","input_schema":{"properties":{"limit":{"default":0,"description":"Maximum number of lines to read (page still capped at ~50KB). Default 0 reads the entire file.","type":"integer"},"offset":{"default":0,"description":"Number of lines to skip from the beginning (1-indexed: offset=N skips the first N lines — continue after line K with offset=K). Default 0 reads from the start.","type":"integer"},"path":{"description":"Path to the file to read. Supports ~/ expansion.","type":"string"}},"required":["path"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"git_blame","description":"Call git_blame for ANY git line-history question — who wrote line N, which commit introduced a bug, why code was written this way. ALWAYS use git_blame over `bash git blame`: it returns structured JSON (no raw-git token waste), guards wrong line numbers / oversized windows, and feeds the blame trail (repeatedly-rewritten lines = bug-prone hot spots). NEVER run `git blame` via bash when git_blame answers the question. Returns {file_path, line_items:[{line_no, commit_hash, author, timestamp, commit_message, code_line}]}. file_path is repo-root-relative (e.g. internal/memory/store.go). Omit start_line/end_line for a small default window (50 lines); ranges wider than 200 lines are refused. Only works on files tracked by git. Optional tool: no git in the environment → structured 'unavailable' error, everything else keeps working.","input_schema":{"properties":{"end_line":{"description":"Last line number; must be \u003e= start_line. Clipped to the file's line count.","type":"integer"},"file_path":{"description":"Repo-root-relative path, e.g. internal/memory/store.go","type":"string"},"start_line":{"description":"First line number (1-based). Omitted = a small default window starting at line 1.","type":"integer"}},"required":["file_path"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"write","description":"Create or fully REPLACE a file's content — existing content is overwritten entirely (no merge). Use for new files and whole-file rewrites. For a targeted change use edit: it sends only the changed lines, so it is cheaper and leaves the rest untouched. Paths support ~/ expansion. If the file exists, its old content is gone after this call. Paths that are symlinks are refused by default to protect the link; pass follow_symlink=true to write through to the target.","input_schema":{"properties":{"content":{"description":"Content to write to the file.","type":"string"},"follow_symlink":{"default":false,"description":"When path is a symlink: false (default) refuses the write (renaming onto a symlink replaces the link node and leaves the target unchanged); true resolves the link and writes the target file in place, keeping the symlink intact.","type":"boolean"},"path":{"description":"Path to the file to write.","type":"string"}},"required":["path","content"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Surgically replace one unique string in a file: finds oldStr and swaps it for newStr. Use edit — NOT write — for targeted changes: only the affected lines are sent (cheaper) and everything else stays intact (safer). oldStr must be unique unless replace_all=true; a non-unique or missing match is refused with the mismatch shown — re-read the file and copy the exact text. Paths that are symlinks are refused by default to protect the link; pass follow_symlink=true to edit through to the target.","input_schema":{"properties":{"follow_symlink":{"default":false,"description":"When path is a symlink: false (default) refuses the edit (renaming onto a symlink replaces the link node and leaves the target unchanged); true resolves the link and edits the target file in place, keeping the symlink intact.","type":"boolean"},"newStr":{"description":"Replacement string.","type":"string"},"oldStr":{"description":"Exact string to find and replace. Must be unique in the file unless replace_all=true.","type":"string"},"path":{"description":"Path to the file to edit.","type":"string"},"replace_all":{"default":false,"description":"Replace ALL occurrences of oldStr. Default false: oldStr must be unique and a non-unique match is refused.","type":"boolean"}},"required":["path","oldStr","newStr"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a shell command via bash -c for build, test, run, git and file operations — NOT for searching code (definitions → code_search, full-text → keyword_search) and NOT for reading files (use read, not cat/head/sed). NEVER invoke grep/rg for a symbol definition — ALWAYS use code_search (one structured call vs a grep round-trip). Reading a file (cat/head/sed -n FILE) when read exists = a logged violation. Returns 'exit=N\\n' + stdout, stderr prefixed '[stderr] '. 120s timeout by default; set timeout_sec only for slightly-long commands. Command may outlive a call? Use job_start — bash kills its WHOLE process group on timeout. Dangerous cmds (rm -rf etc.) blocked unless GOPI_ALLOW_DANGEROUS=1.","input_schema":{"properties":{"command":{"description":"Shell command to execute.","type":"string"},"timeout_sec":{"default":120,"description":"Max seconds to wait. Default 120. 0 = unlimited absolute time (still subject to the 120s no-output safety net).","type":"integer"},"workdir":{"description":"Working directory for the command (must exist). Omitted = current directory.","type":"string"}},"required":["command"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"code_search","description":"Locate where a symbol (function, struct, interface, var, const, type, class, macro) is DEFINED. ALWAYS use this for a definition lookup — NEVER run `bash grep \u003csymbol\u003e` for it. ONE call returns every definition as structured hits (file:line, kind, signature) from a pre-indexed ctags database (Go, Python, shell, C/C++ incl. .h headers, Rust, JavaScript, TypeScript), auto-falling back to ripgrep. When reviewing or reading unfamiliar code, resolve EVERY unknown identifier here FIRST — before grep, before guessing from includes. Faster and cleaner than bash grep: one structured call vs a 2-call grep round-trip of noisy lines you must re-read+filter. Takes a symbol NAME (an identifier like 'NewClient') — never a sentence, description or tool name. 'No symbol found' means the identifier exists nowhere in the codebase: do not grep for it; use keyword_search for related text or files outside the indexed languages.","input_schema":{"properties":{"symbol":{"description":"Symbol name to look up (e.g. 'NewClient', 'Thing').","type":"string"}},"required":["symbol"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"keyword_search","description":"Full-text search via ripgrep over any file — code, docs, comments, config, scripts. Query may be a plain string or a regex. Returns structured hits (file:line, snippet). Use this for USAGES/references of an identifier (where is X called?), error messages, comments, docs, config — and as the follow-up when code_search reports a symbol is not found. For locating a symbol DEFINITION use code_search, not grep. Too-broad results? Narrow the query — noisy output wastes a round.","input_schema":{"properties":{"query":{"description":"Text or regex to search for.","type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"web_search","description":"Search the web or ask an AI; returns unified JSON ({status,count,results}). Methods: bing (CN direct, fastest), google (overseas/EN), arxiv (papers), deepseek (AI answer), github (repos/issues; code needs a token), wiki (Wikipedia). No method arg = bing+google+arxiv merged (dedup + relevance sort + source attribution). count caps results (default 5, max 20); ignored for the AI-ask methods. Snippets only — follow up with web_fetch for the full page. Use web_search — NOT bash curl of a search engine page or shelling out to the plugin scripts.","input_schema":{"properties":{"count":{"default":5,"description":"Max results to return (default 5, max 20).","type":"integer"},"method":{"description":"bing|google|arxiv|deepseek|github|wiki. Empty = merged multi-method default.","enum":["bing","google","arxiv","deepseek","github","wiki"],"type":"string"},"query":{"description":"Search phrase or question.","type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"web_fetch","description":"Fetch a URL and return its cleaned full text as JSON ({status,url,chars,truncated,content}). Search ≠ Read: web_search returns snippets — use web_fetch to actually read a page (deep research, verifying a source, reading a README/abstract in full). Takes ONE http(s) URL (e.g. a web_search result URL); boilerplate HTML (nav/header/footer/scripts) is stripped and the text is tail-truncated to max_chars (default 8000) to keep tokens low. Use web_fetch — NOT bash curl of a webpage. Workflow: web_search to discover URLs → web_fetch to read the promising ones.","input_schema":{"properties":{"max_chars":{"default":8000,"description":"Max chars of cleaned text to return (default 8000).","type":"integer"},"url":{"description":"http(s) URL to fetch (e.g. a web_search result URL, GitHub README, arxiv abstract page).","type":"string"}},"required":["url"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"memory_search","description":"Search gopi's distilled knowledge base (hippocampus) for PAST experience: success paths, pitfalls, lessons. USE FIRST at the start of every task, when stuck, or when the task looks done before — a known recipe beats re-deriving it (5 分钟 vs 重造轮子). SKIP off-topic chatter / weather-like questions: value-gated retrieval, not dumb RAG. Returns records with tier / confidence / lesson_type / when_at signals — judge each hit's worth yourself. After seeing results, immediately assess each hit: used it → memory_feedback(useful); CONTRADICTS your results → memory_feedback with rating=useless and the originating tool_use_id — the harness will drop it from context right away — then memory_update to fix it (误导=给未来的自己挖坑). NOTE: tool_use_id is the id of THIS search call (the call_… on the tool_use block), NOT the record number #N — the result header stamps it ('tool_use id: …'); copy it verbatim. Empty result = nothing distilled yet: after the task, memory_store what you learned (取不到 → 酿造). Hits ranked by query-token coverage + tier + staleness; cold-tier rows excluded.","input_schema":{"properties":{"intent":{"description":"Optional routing hint for the backend (e.g. 'debug', 'build', 'search').","type":"string"},"limit":{"default":5,"description":"Max records to return (default 5).","type":"integer"},"query":{"description":"What you want to know: a task, problem or situation you suspect happened before.","type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"memory_store","description":"Save a DISTILLED lesson into the knowledge base (hippocampus) so future tasks can find it. USE after a task with a reusable lesson: a success path that worked, a pitfall, a pattern worth repeating. USE MID-TASK too, the moment a notable fact emerges — do not wait for task end (task end is the floor, not the ceiling): 上下文随时可能重置, 未记录=丢失. Store only DISTILLED experience — repeatable pattern / reproducible loop / measurable value. NEVER store raw conversation transcripts or step-by-step logs (流水账). Structure: topic (the subject), question (what a future agent might search for), answer (how to succeed / what NOT to do). YOUR JOB = leave retrieval breadcrumbs (检索面包屑): 2-3 mixed 中英 keywords per 5W1H dimension (WHAT 主题 / WHY 问题 / WHEN 场景 / WHERE 环境 / WHO 实体 / HOW 方法), with synonyms / translations / variant phrasings / Unicode normalization (agent‑loop → agent-loop) — these ARE the breadcrumbs memory_search will match; think one step further when storing. Returns the stored record id; deduped means it merged into an existing entry.","input_schema":{"properties":{"answer":{"description":"The distilled experience: how to succeed / what not to do.","type":"string"},"keywords":{"description":"5W1H 检索面包屑：中英混合关键词（主题/问题/场景/环境/实体/方法六维度），含同义/翻译/变体问法/Unicode 归一化","items":{"type":"string"},"type":"array"},"name":{"description":"type=procedural only: workflow name (unique identity, e.g. '读错误日志→定位→修复→验证').","type":"string"},"preconditions":{"description":"type=procedural only: preconditions that must hold before running the workflow.","type":"string"},"question":{"description":"A question a future agent might ask when it needs this lesson.","type":"string"},"source":{"description":"Optional provenance (repo, file, task).","type":"string"},"steps":{"description":"type=procedural only: ordered step list (each a command or instruction). Structured JSON is persisted; prefer 3-8 actionable steps.","items":{"type":"string"},"type":"array"},"tools_used":{"description":"type=procedural only: tools the workflow exercises (e.g. bash, code_search).","items":{"type":"string"},"type":"array"},"topic":{"description":"Subject of the lesson.","type":"string"},"trigger":{"description":"type=procedural only: 何时启用 — the condition/situation that should activate this workflow.","type":"string"},"type":{"description":"Row type: declarative (default) = distilled lesson QA; procedural = reusable workflow stored in the procedural table. With type=procedural, pass name/trigger/steps (and optionally preconditions/tools_used) — topic/question/answer are ignored except as fallbacks (topic→name, question→trigger, answer→single step).","enum":["declarative","procedural"],"type":"string"}},"required":["topic","question","answer"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"memory_feedback","description":"Vote on a memory_search hit RIGHT NOW — 投票=质量裁判, judge each result the moment it arrives (搜索结果的当下就投), never defer to task end. Record an explicit useful/useless vote on a memory_search hit so the knowledge base evolves (LLM 是唯一用户 = 唯一质量裁判 — you are already judging each hit while reading it; this tool just lands that judgment). useful = you actually used it. useless ONLY when the hit CONTRADICTS your operation results / is provably stale or wrong (bug-report culture: a merely-meh hit still gets useful or no vote). Then memory_update to correct the wrong memory — feedback says WHAT is wrong, update does the FIX (右转: call memory_update with id + the corrected field RIGHT NOW, not later). One vote per session per hit; each moves the score ≤0.2; ≥2 useless votes auto-demote to cold (reversible). When rating=useless, pass the originating search's tool_use_id (the call_… id stamped in that search result's header, NOT the record #N) — the harness then drops that search's tool_use + tool_result pair from context immediately (the useless hit stops being re-sent every round).","input_schema":{"properties":{"id":{"description":"Record id from a memory_search result (the #N in each hit's meta line).","type":"integer"},"rating":{"description":"useful = adopted/helped; useless = contradicted/failed/misleading; unused = seen but not used (neutral).","enum":["useful","useless","unused"],"type":"string"},"reason":{"description":"Optional short reason (recommended for useless votes).","type":"string"},"tool_use_id":{"description":"Optional tool_use_id of the originating memory_search call (the call_… string from that tool_use block — NOT the record id #N). When rating=useless, the harness removes that search's tool_use + tool_result pair from context immediately.","type":"string"}},"required":["id","rating"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"memory_update","description":"Directly correct or re-tier an existing memory record (hard edit — the soft signal is memory_feedback). USE at the CONTRADICTION moment: a search hit contradicted by your VERIFIED evidence (operation output), or a record you know is stale (API / behavior changed) — fix it NOW, don't let the wrong record sit (误导=给未来的自己挖坑). Prefer memory_feedback when unsure — feedback is reversible and cheap; update is for certainty. A corrected record stays hot/warm; a wrong record left warm poisons every later task. Do NOT use update to delete history — the knowledge base keeps versions for audit. Input: id (the #N) + the CORRECTED field(s): rewrite the wrong field's value (topic/question/answer/tier/keywords/confidence), not a patch-note stuffed into keywords.","input_schema":{"properties":{"answer":{"description":"New distilled experience body (highest confidence bar — content-level rewrite).","type":"string"},"confidence":{"description":"New confidence in [0,1].","type":"number"},"id":{"description":"Record id from a memory_search result (the #N in each hit's meta line). Only records you retrieved may be updated.","type":"integer"},"keywords":{"description":"Replace 5W1H 检索面包屑 (rewrites search_kw AND the answer's keywords tail).","items":{"type":"string"},"type":"array"},"question":{"description":"New question a future agent might ask.","type":"string"},"source":{"description":"New provenance (repo, file, task).","type":"string"},"tier":{"description":"cold = demote (no longer 1st-tier to return). Prefer demotion over content edits for low-quality records.","enum":["hot","warm","cold"],"type":"string"},"topic":{"description":"New subject (replaces the record's topic).","type":"string"}},"required":["id"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"context_trim","description":"TRIM DEAD-ENDS NOW — 干净仪表盘: the moment a tool result is a dead end, trim it immediately. A dead end is an errored/failed/no-value output — bash command on a missing file or wrong args, a read of a nonexistent path, a tool result with no useful signal. Every untrimmed dead end is re-sent to the model every round — trimming is how you stop paying for it. ALWAYS trim before moving on; NEVER leave an untrimmed dead-end result in context. Call with the dead end call's id; optionally pass replace_with to leave a one-line conclusion (e.g. 'X failed because Y — don't retry'): with replace_with the pair is KEPT, the bulky result swapped for your conclusion; without it the pair is removed entirely. The id is the call_… string stamped in that tool result's header ('tool_use id: …') — copy it verbatim, NOT the record number #N. Each id is trimmed at most once; unknown ids no-op silently.","input_schema":{"properties":{"call_id":{"description":"The tool_use id of the dead-end call to trim (the call_… string stamped in that tool result's header — copy it verbatim).","type":"string"},"replace_with":{"description":"Optional one-line conclusion that REPLACES the original tool result (keeps the pair, drops the bulky output — 'X failed because Y — don't retry'). Omit to delete the pair entirely.","type":"string"}},"required":["call_id"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"task_evaluate","description":"Evaluate a work request FIRST: trivially doable in THIS session → do it directly (no task); too big / multi-stage / out of current scope → call task_submit to enqueue it (divide-and-conquer). Routing gate: 1) trivially doable = local files + a few tool rounds, done within this conversation — the DEFAULT, just start working; 2) enqueue-worthy = needs many rounds of separate work (a full feature with tests+docs), a bug you found but cannot fix without derailing the current task (tags=['bug']), or a split-off subtask of a bigger job (parent_task_id). Value over just doing it: enqueued tasks run in a FRESH gopi with full context budget and session/memory/tooling — they do not bloat or derail this session; a bug submitted now gets fixed by another instance while you keep working. Blockers: do NOT submit work that only makes sense mid-conversation (e.g. 'reply to the user') — this queue executes via gopi task run/serve with the payload as the sole briefing.","input_schema":{"properties":{"request":{"description":"The work request text to evaluate.","type":"string"}},"required":["request"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"task_submit","description":"Submit a work item to the persistent task queue (status=pending) instead of doing it in this session. USE WHEN: 1) you discover a bug you cannot fix without derailing the current task — submit it with tags=['bug'] and payload=reproduction steps + log paths, then CONTINUE your current work (the queue/another instance picks it up); 2) the current task is too large — split it: submit each subtask with parent_task_id=\u003ccurrent task id\u003e and a self-contained payload briefing (divide-and-conquer; siblings execute serially via gopi task serve); 3) work that only makes sense asynchronously/for others. Repo closure: work targeting ANOTHER repo (a foreign project) must carry that repo — the repo parameter stamps deliverable.repo and a runner picking the task up without one only gets a cross-project warning at claim time (verify-before-execute is on you). Every bug-tagged submit prints a running [bug-flow] tally (bug tasks total / verified / reopened) — the adoption loop's measurable heartbeat. ROUTE FIRST: trivially-doable work should just be done — task_evaluate is the decision gate; task_submit is its 'cannot do it here' exit. Anti-self-reference: this session never executes its own submissions (claims exclude the submitting session) — expect the task_id in the result and reference it via bash `gopi task show \u003ctask_id\u003e`.","input_schema":{"properties":{"parent_task_id":{"description":"Optional: id of the parent task when this submission splits one (divide-and-conquer tree).","type":"string"},"payload":{"description":"FULL request text — the briefing the executing gopi will run with as its only input. Write it self-contained: context, goal, constraints, acceptance. Omit only when the summary says it all.","type":"string"},"priority":{"description":"Optional queue weight, lower runs first (M4.11). Omit to derive from tags: bug→1 (highest — LLM-reported bugs are bugs), docs/doc-only→8, else 5. An explicit value ALWAYS overrides the tags derivation.","type":"integer"},"repo":{"description":"Optional git repo path/URL the work happens in. DEFAULT: your current working directory — the normal case, do NOT invent a repo path. The path is verified (git rev-parse) BEFORE the task row is written: a non-git directory aborts the whole submit with an error. Verified repo lands in the task's deliverable block (deliverable.repo) so verify/merge run with zero memory.","type":"string"},"summary":{"description":"One-line ledger headline (what a human scanning `gopi task list` should read).","type":"string"},"tags":{"description":"Optional classification tags; 'bug' is the reserved bug-flow tag (payload = repro steps + log paths).","items":{"type":"string"},"type":"array"}},"required":["summary"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"job_start","description":"Start a shell command in the BACKGROUND, detached in its own session — immune to bash timeouts (bash kills its whole process group on timeout, which would take a shared background process down with it). Returns a job id + stdout/stderr file paths. Use for long-running commands (builds, tests, servers, scripts). Do NOT poll: continue other work — the harness injects a notice when the job finishes; collect the output once with job_output.","input_schema":{"properties":{"command":{"description":"Shell command to run in the background.","type":"string"}},"required":["command"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"write_task_area","description":"Commit the CURRENT subtask/item's stable conclusion into the session working area (in-session analog of the task-ledger writeback; POC-decisive: a submit tool forces one-item-at-a-time execution — 9/9 vs 0/3 free-text on a 5-item chain). CALL IT ONCE PER ITEM, the moment that item converges — never batch several items into one call, never free-text 'all done' prose. Schema: task_id (which item), status=\"completed\" only, conclusion REQUIRED (compact final result/decision/key facts — what a later step must know), compressed_analysis OPTIONAL (omit = discard the analysis; include only when a later item must reuse it). After a successful call the item's working region is COMMITTED: the framework records the conclusion (append-only, session-scoped, queryable) and may drop/trim that item's working frame on the next round — the conclusion is carried forward as authoritative context. Do NOT repeat or re-derive a committed item's analysis later — refer to its recorded conclusion. This tool is SESSION-internal: it does NOT touch the cross-session task ledger (task_submit/task_evaluate are separate).","input_schema":{"properties":{"compressed_analysis":{"description":"Optional. Omit (default) = the analysis is DISCARDED after commit. Include a compressed version only when a later item must reuse the reasoning.","type":"string"},"conclusion":{"description":"REQUIRED compact final result / decision / key facts of this item — what a later step must know without re-reading this item's analysis.","type":"string"},"status":{"description":"Fixed value \"completed\" (only status accepted).","type":"string"},"task_id":{"description":"The subtask/item id being committed (e.g. \"1\", \"2\" … matching your task list).","type":"string"}},"required":["task_id","status","conclusion"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"job_output","description":"Fetch a background job's status and output: status=running | exited code=N | killed, plus the captured output (stderr included). Call ONCE — never busy-poll or sleep-wait; work on other things while the job runs (you are notified on completion). Every fetch re-sends the full output: read once, don't re-fetch.","input_schema":{"properties":{"job_id":{"description":"Job id returned by job_start.","type":"string"}},"required":["job_id"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"job_kill","description":"Kill a background job started by job_start — kills its WHOLE process group, child processes included. Use when the job is no longer needed or must be stopped. Prefer killing over leaving zombie jobs holding resources.","input_schema":{"properties":{"job_id":{"description":"Job id returned by job_start.","type":"string"}},"required":["job_id"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"todo","description":"Show the current task list state as tracked by the harness (from your [TASK_LIST_BEGIN]...[TASK_LIST_END] blocks). Read-only and zero-cost: use it to see where you stand instead of narrating progress in thinking; you update tasks by emitting the TASK_LIST block in your response. Check before reporting progress, choosing the next step, and at status checkpoints.","input_schema":{"properties":{},"required":[],"type":"object"}},{"name":"vision_analyze","description":"Understand an image via the vision model (deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp — 图片理解/OCR/图表分析). When to use: 图片理解 / 截图文字识别 / 图表分析 — 截图文字识别直接调 vision，不需要 OCR 脚本 (replaces external OCR). For RENDER verification (font/glyph quality) use pixel-level comparison (IoU), not vision — a vision model can't quantify quality loss. Input: image_path (local file, required) + prompt (optional, default 描述这张图片并识别文字). The image is read, base64-encoded and sent to the vision model in one lightweight standalone request — 图片不进主上下文，只有分析结果文本返回 (context protection). Supports jpg/jpeg/png/gif/webp, max 32 MiB. Images are only valid in user messages — this tool always sends them as a user message.","input_schema":{"properties":{"image_path":{"description":"Path to the local image file to analyze (required). Supported formats: jpg/jpeg, png, gif, webp; max 32 MiB.","type":"string"},"prompt":{"description":"Optional instruction for the vision model (e.g. '识别图片中的所有中文文字', '描述图表数据'). Default: 描述这张图片，并识别其中的文字（如有）.","type":"string"}},"required":["image_path"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"text2img","description":"Render a text file to PNG images (纯 GO 零依赖 TrueType 渲染 — text→PNG for screenshots / reports / OCR-friendly images; CJK via autodetected system font). When to use: 把文本变成图片 / 生成截图素材 / 渲染含中文的图文 — internal/text2img 自研 SFNT/TrueType 解析 + 4x 超采样栅格化, 无 CGO 无 x/image. Verify render quality with PIXEL-level comparison (IoU), not OCR — OCR can't see quality loss. Input: src (text file path, required) + out (PNG path, optional — default \u003csrc\u003e.png) + optional size/cols/lh/lines/margin/font overrides (defaults: 20px 字号, 50 列折行, 32 行高, 90 行/页, 12 边距). Text longer than one page auto-paginates into out-1.png, out-2.png, … Returns the page/chars/lines summary and output paths.","input_schema":{"properties":{"cols":{"description":"Wrap width in characters for over-long lines (default 50).","type":"integer"},"font":{"description":"Font path override (default: autodetect system CJK TrueType font).","type":"string"},"lh":{"description":"Line height in px, ~font-size×1.6 (default 32).","type":"integer"},"lines":{"description":"Max lines per page (~2800 chars/page; default 90).","type":"integer"},"margin":{"description":"Page margin in px (default 12).","type":"integer"},"out":{"description":"Output PNG path (optional). Default: \u003csrc\u003e.png; multi-page output appends -1, -2, …","type":"string"},"size":{"description":"Font size in px (default 20).","type":"integer"},"src":{"description":"Path to the input text file to render (required).","type":"string"}},"required":["src"],"type":"object"},"cache_control":{"type":"ephemeral"}}],"stream":true,"thinking":{"type":"enabled","budget_tokens":8192},"reasoning_effort":"high"}
